@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Octo 18: control systems, dates and files)

Three smaller families: transfer functions, calendar arithmetic, and reading and writing files.

@section(Transfer functions)

`tf` builds a system from numerator and denominator; `step` and `bode` draw its responses.

@octo
sys = tf(1, [1 3 2])
pole(sys)', dcgain(sys)
[y, t] = step(sys); round(y(end) * 100) / 100
step(sys); title("step response");
figure; bode(sys);
s = ss(sys); s.A
cl = feedback(sys, 1)
@end

@section(Dates and times)

Dates are day numbers, so arithmetic on them is ordinary arithmetic.

@octo
d = datenum(2026, 1, 2);
datestr(d), datestr(d + 30, "yyyy-mm-dd")
weekday(d)
v = datevec(d); v(1:3)
calendar(2026, 2)
numel(clock), cputime >= 0, ischar(date)
@end

@section(Files)

A block can write a file into its own workspace and read it back in the same run.

@octo
M = magic(3);
csvwrite("m.csv", M);
csvread("m.csv")
dlmwrite("m.txt", M, " ");
dlmread("m.txt")(2, :)
fid = fopen("note.txt", "w"); fprintf(fid, "line one\nline two\n"); fclose(fid);
txt = fileread("note.txt")
fid = fopen("note.txt"); first = fgetl(fid), fclose(fid);
T = table([1; 2], [3; 4]); writetable(T, "t.csv"); readtable("t.csv")
exist("note.txt", "file")
@end
